## Layer 2: Frame loop + ProgressObserver

### Game-engine style frame loop

The core rendering primitive is a generic update→render loop, similar to
a game engine's main loop. This decouples execution events from display
timing — the renderer runs at its own frame rate.

```rust
trait FrameLoop {
    /// Process pending state changes (from observer events).
    fn update(&mut self, progress: &DagProgress, dt: Duration);
    /// Produce a visual frame from current state.
    fn render(&mut self, progress: &DagProgress);
}
```

#### Frame scheduling policy

Different situations need different policies for how the frame loop
relates to execution:

```rust
enum FramePolicy {
    /// Always show latest state. Skip intermediate frames if execution
    /// outpaces the frame rate. Display never lags behind execution.
    /// Best for: fast pipelines, CI, non-interactive.
    Latest,

    /// Sequential: show every state transition, even if execution has
    /// moved ahead. Queue transitions and replay them at frame rate.
    /// Best for: dynamic mode where you want to see every animation.
    Sequential { max_queue: usize },

    /// Adaptive: show latest by default, but slow down when transitions
    /// are visually interesting (edge flow, node morph). Speeds back up
    /// when execution is idle or done.
    /// Best for: interactive terminal with dynamic mode.
    Adaptive { min_fps: u8, max_fps: u8 },
}
```

#### Execution ↔ frame loop interaction

```
    execute_flat() loop              Frame loop (separate concern)
    ┌──────────────────┐            ┌──────────────────────────┐
    │ for node in topo │  events    │ loop {                   │
    │   observer.on_*()│──────────→ │   update(progress, dt)   │
    │   execute(node)  │            │   render(progress)       │
    │ end              │            │   sleep(frame_interval)  │
    └──────────────────┘            │ }                        │
                                    └──────────────────────────┘
```

For synchronous execution (current), the frame loop runs between node
executions — update+render after each observer callback. For future
concurrent execution, the frame loop runs on its own thread/tick.

### ProgressObserver trait

Formalizes the existing CiContext ad-hoc callback pattern into a trait:

```rust
trait ProgressObserver: Send {
    fn on_dag_start(&mut self, snapshot: &DagSnapshot);
    fn on_node_start(&mut self, node_id: &NodeId);
    fn on_node_complete(&mut self, node_id: &NodeId, summary: OutputSummary);
    fn on_node_failed(&mut self, node_id: &NodeId, error: &str);
    fn on_node_skipped(&mut self, node_id: &NodeId);
    fn on_node_intercepted(&mut self, node_id: &NodeId, summary: OutputSummary);
    fn on_dag_complete(&mut self, elapsed: Duration);
}
```

### DagSnapshot — reuses existing types

```rust
struct DagSnapshot {
    node_ids: Vec<NodeId>,
    edges: Vec<Edge>,                    // Reuse existing Edge type
    topo_order: Vec<NodeId>,
    boundary_nodes: HashSet<NodeId>,     // From detect_boundaries()
    labels: HashMap<NodeId, String>,
}
```

### OutputSummary

Extracts summarization logic from existing `print_log_entry()`:

```rust
struct OutputSummary { fields: Vec<FieldSummary>, elapsed: Duration }
struct FieldSummary { name: String, kind: FieldKind, preview: String }
enum FieldKind { Scalar, Number, Boolean, List(usize), Map(usize), Secret, Url, Empty }
```

FieldKind adds semantic categorization on top of Value's structural types
(e.g., detecting URLs within Str values).

